<Page title={"About"} description={"Romain Blachier, energy and geopolitics expert: oil, electricity, Taiwan, Africa. 18 years in energy, 12 years in elected office in Lyon, lecturer since 2010."} lang="en">
	<h1 class="page-title">About</h1>
	<p class="page-lede">Energy and geopolitics, around one question: who really decides, and with what means.</p>
	<div class="page-body ip-split">
<section class="ip-sec ip-sec--intro">
<div class="ip-sec-body">
<p>I work on energy policy and the geopolitics of energy, from OPEC+ oil to power grids, with two main fields: Central Africa and the Indo-Pacific, Taiwan in particular (energy security, semiconductors, China-Taiwan relations). French nuclear power in a heatwave, straits and oil rent are part of my subjects too.</p>
<p>One thread runs through all of it: the gap between the authority an institution holds on paper and its actual capacity to act. That holds for a regulator, for a metropolitan authority, for a national utility.</p>
</div>
</section>

<dl class="hp-stats ip-stats">
	<div class="hp-stat"><dt>18 years</dt><dd>in renewable electricity</dd></div>
	<div class="hp-stat"><dt>12 years</dt><dd>in local executive office in Lyon</dd></div>
	<div class="hp-stat"><dt>16 years</dt><dd>of university teaching</dd></div>
	<div class="hp-stat"><dt>3 regions</dt><dd>French-speaking Africa, Europe, Indo-Pacific</dd></div>
</dl>

<section class="ip-sec">
<h2>Background</h2>
<div class="ip-sec-body">
<ul class="ip-list">
<li><strong>Energy.</strong> Eighteen years in the renewable electricity sector (since 2008), currently as an innovation project manager.</li>
<li><strong>Local government.</strong> Twelve years of executive office in the City and then the Metropolitan area of Lyon (2008-2020): culture, digital, international.</li>
<li><strong>Teaching.</strong> Since 2010, some 180 hours a year, in French, English and Spanish. In 2026-2027: {taught}. Scheduled for 2026-2027: {upcoming}. Plus an annual guest lecture series in Spanish at the Universidad de Granada.</li>
<li><strong>Research.</strong> Work on the governance of the electricity transition: an article in <em>La Revue de l'Énergie</em> (2026), an article accepted by the journal <em>Flux</em> on Kinmen's electricity supply, and a paper accepted at the 10th AIEE Energy Symposium (Turin, November 2026).</li>
<li><strong>Institutions.</strong> Associate fellow at the Fondation Jean-Jaurès, member of the civil-society college of the Plateforme RSE (France Stratégie), energy expert listed with the African Development Bank since 2025 and registered on the Asian Development Bank's consultant roster since 2026.</li>
<li><strong>Taiwan.</strong> President of the France-Formosa Association since 2012; two official missions to Taiwan at the invitation of the Ministry of Foreign Affairs (2019 and 2025).</li>
<li><strong>Advisory and training.</strong> Assignments and training delivered through Lapin Bleu, an independent firm based in Lyon and a registered training provider. <a href="/en/consulting">See assignments</a>.</li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>

<section class="ip-sec">
<h2>Fieldwork</h2>
<div class="ip-sec-body">
<p>Three regions of work: French-speaking Africa, Europe, the Indo-Pacific. Fieldwork in Cameroon, Cambodia, Ukraine and Taiwan. In July 2026, a mission on the power sector reform of a Central African state, followed by a lecture at an international relations institute in the region. What I see in the field ends up in a lecture, then in an article.</p>
</div>
</section>

<section class="ip-sec">
<h2>Areas of expertise</h2>
<div class="ip-sec-body">
<p>Energy markets and geopolitics (oil and OPEC+, LNG, straits), energy transition and sovereignty, nuclear power and climate adaptation, Taiwan &amp; Indo-Pacific, African energy systems, local governance, comparative political systems, strategic and crisis communications, public affairs.</p>
</div>
</section>

<section class="ip-sec">
<h2>Languages</h2>
<div class="ip-sec-body">
<p>French (native), English (C1, working language for fieldwork, writing and teaching), Spanish (C1, teaching language: lectures on European politics at the Universidad de Granada), basic Mandarin Chinese.</p>
</div>
</section>

